  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe address the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by extending the given parent solution with several novel components:\n- Smoothed cross-correlation objective that penalizes boundary effects\n- Warm-starts via resolution interpolation\n- Progressive smoothing to escape local optima\n- Elimination of non-positive shifts\n\nThe core idea is to transform the optimization landscape to make it harder to converge to suboptimal solutions by:\n  - Using a global expansion factor \u00df to smooth cross-correlation terms\n  - Implementing last-value projection for resolution scaling\n  - Allocating computational budgets based on gradient impact\n\nThe primary limitation is computational scaling with resolution. This approach improves upon the parent by:\n  \u2022 Explicitly incorporating integral constraints into all correlation calculations\n  \u2022 Using gradient-targeted computation time allocation\n  \u2022 Maintaining dimension-independent cross-resolution consistency\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nWe define the optimization as:\n\nMinimize: C_\u03b4 = max_k ( \u2211\u1d62 h\u1d62 (1 - h_{i+k}) )/L\u2081\n\nSubject to:\n  0 \u2264 h\u1d62 \u2264 1 \u2200i\n  \u2211\u1d62 h\u1d62 = N/2  (where N = number of resolution points)\n\nKey transformations:\n- Smoothed correlation: terms penalized by |k|/5 + \u03b4 where \u03b4=0.02\n- Low-pass filter: project_h(h, \u03bc) applies boxcar smoothing of width \u03bc\n- Progressive morphing: interpolate_2ton(h, N) converts between resolutions\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n- Validate input resolution N from parent transfer (n=320 initially)\n- Compute initial integral budget: n/2 = target_sum\n- Compute synchronous projections:\n      corr_full = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n      C\u2080 = max(corr_full) * dx  (dx=2.0/N)\n      enforced = project_h(h, target_sum)\n\n- Parallelized coordinate descent:\n  n_workers = 4  # Cores to use for coordinate optimization\n  solver = OptimizeCore(target_sum, N, h)\n\n- Progressive smoothing: scale solution by last-value projection\n- Adaptive resolution (transfer):\n    if new_N:\n        h_new = interpolate_2ton(h, target_resolution)\n        target_sum_new = project_h_vals(h_new)   # not explicitly reduced\n        h_projected = broadcast_downscale(h, new_N)\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n- Primary Parameter Sweep:\n    \u00df_range = [0, 0.1, 0.5, 2, 5]   # Deterministic smoothing factor\n    \u03b1_range = [0.1, 0.3, 0.6, 0.8]   # Projection ball radius\n    \u03bc_schedule = 0.01 * exp(-10.0/1000)  # Scheduled smoothing decay\n\n- Progressive Resolution Adaptation:\n    resolve_levels = [320, 512, 768]  # Tripling the resolution each time\n    min_samples = 16  # Minimum projections for feasibility\n    scaled_resolutions = [m * len(parent) / 320 for m in [1, 1.5, 2]]\n\n- Energy allocation strategy:\n    time_allocation = {\"gp\": 0.3, \"serial\": 0.5, \"cc\": 0.2}  # Fraction of time per accelerator\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n- Use gradient-based time allocation:\n    wt_active = vectorized_gradient_magnitude  # Compute gradient norms\n    budgets = (budget_s * min_exec_time) * wt_active/sum(wt_active)\n    maxiter = max(200, round(budgets / (task_time * wt_active)))\n\n- Fallback mechanism:\n    def fail_safe(h_cur):\n        clip_soft(h_cur, 0.05)  # 5% soft clipping\n        apply_coordinator = adjust_mean(h_cur, target_sum)\n        return cross_morph(np.random.rand(N))\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- Validation checks:\n    if any(h[i] < 0 or h[i] > 1):  raise ValueError(\"Out-of-bounds values\") \n    if abs(sum(h) - target_sum) > 1e-5:  raise ValueError(\"Integral mismatch\")\n    \u03bb_valid = np.mean(corr_full**2) / (3.0*\u03c3)  # Must be in valid range [100, 120]\n\n- Final candidate must satisfy:\n    \u2022 Integral constraint: sum(h) = n/2 (within 1e-6)\n    \u2022 Boundary bounds: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 (gradient-based clipping)\n    \u2022 Correlation: max(corr_full) > 0.0\n\nAll values for L\u2080=320 are derived directly from the parent vector.",
